Since <imath>AD:DC=1:2</imath> thus <imath>\triangle ABD=\frac{1}{3} \cdot 360 = 120.</imath>

Similarly, <imath>\triangle DBC = \frac{2}{3} \cdot 360 = 240.</imath>

Now, since <imath>E</imath> is a midpoint of <imath>BD</imath>, <imath>\triangle ABE = \triangle AED = 120 \div 2 = 60.</imath>

We can use the fact that <imath>E</imath> is a midpoint of <imath>BD</imath> even further. Connect lines <imath>E</imath> and <imath>C</imath> so that <imath>\triangle BEC</imath> and <imath>\triangle DEC</imath> share 2 sides.

We know that <imath>\triangle BEC=\triangle DEC=240 \div 2 = 120</imath> since <imath>E</imath> is a midpoint of <imath>BD.</imath>

Let's label <imath>\triangle BEF</imath> <imath>x</imath>. We know that <imath>\triangle EFC</imath> is <imath>120-x</imath> since <imath>\triangle BEC = 120.</imath>

Note that with this information now, we can deduct more things that are needed to finish the solution.

Note that <imath>\frac{EF}{AE} = \frac{120-x}{180} = \frac{x}{60}.</imath> because of triangles <imath>EBF, ABE, AEC,</imath> and <imath>EFC.</imath>

We want to find <imath>x.</imath>

This is a simple equation, and solving we get <imath>x=\boxed{\textbf{(B)}30}.</imath>

