==Solution 17==
Since <imath>AD:DC=1:2</imath> and triangles <imath>ADB</imath> and <imath>CDB</imath> have the same height, we would have <imath>[\triangle ADB]:[\triangle DCB]=1:2</imath>. Since the area of <imath>\triangle ABC=360</imath> and <imath>[\triangle ADB]:[\triangle DCB]=1:2</imath>, we must have <imath>[\triangle ADB]=120</imath> and <imath>[\triangle DCB]=240</imath>. Since <imath>E</imath> is the midpoint of side <imath>BD</imath> of triangle <imath>ABD</imath>, we would have <imath>[\triangle ADE]=[\triangle AEB]</imath>. Since <imath>[\triangle ADE]+[\triangle AEB]=[\triangle ADB]</imath> and <imath>[\triangle ADB=120</imath>, we have <imath>[\triangle ADE]+[\triangle AEB]=120</imath>. Since <imath>[\triangle ADE]+[\triangle AEB]=120</imath> and <imath>[\triangle ADE]=[\triangle AEB]</imath>, we must have <imath>[\triangle ADE]=[\triangle AEB]=60</imath>. Now, notice that <imath>[\triangle EBF]=[\triangle AFB]-[\triangle AEB]=[\triangle AFB]-60</imath> so let us find <imath>[\triangle AFB]</imath>. Since triangles <imath>AFB</imath> and <imath>AFC</imath> have the same height, the ratio of their areas is the ratio of their bases, so <imath>[\triangle AFB]:[\triangle AFC]=FB:FC</imath>. By Menelaus's Theorem for triangle <imath>BDC</imath> and the transversal that goes through the points <imath>A</imath>, <imath>E</imath>, and <imath>F</imath>, we have <cmath>\frac{CA}{AD}\times\frac{DE}{EB}\times\frac{BF}{FC}=1.</cmath> Since <imath>E</imath> is the midpoint of <imath>BD</imath>, we'd have <imath>\frac{DE}{EB}=1</imath>. Since <imath>AD:DC=1:2</imath>, we'd have <cmath>\frac{CA}{AD}=\frac{AD+DC}{AD}=\frac{AD}{AD}+\frac{DC}{AD}=1+\frac{1}{\frac{AD}{DC}}=1+\frac{1}{\frac12}=1+2=3.</cmath> Since <imath>\frac{CA}{AD}=3</imath> and <imath>\frac{DE}{EB}=1</imath>, plugging into Menelaus's Theorem gives <imath>3\times1\times\frac{BF}{FC}=1</imath>, so <imath>\frac{BF}{FC}=\frac13</imath>. <imath>\frac{BF}{FC}=\frac13</imath> is the same as <imath>FB:FC=1:3</imath>, so <imath>[\triangle AFB]:[\triangle AFC]=1:3</imath>. Since <imath>[\triangle AFB]:[\triangle AFC]=1:3</imath> and <imath>[\triangle AFB]+[\triangle AFC]=[\triangle ABC]=360</imath>, we must have <imath>[\triangle AFB]=90</imath> and <imath>[\triangle AFC]=270</imath>. Since we have <imath>[\triangle AFB]=90</imath> and we also noticed that <imath>[\triangle EBF]=[\triangle AFB]-60</imath>, we have <imath>[\triangle EBF]=90-60=30</imath>, so the area of <imath>\triangle EBF</imath> is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(B) }30}</imath>.

